<Project>

  <!-- P16b / C-SUP-02 (OQ-027): pin patched transitive versions so the CI dependency gate can block on
       High/Critical CVEs.

       ⚠⚠ THREE PINS WERE REMOVED HERE BY DW-080 PHASE A, AND THE REMOVAL IS EVIDENCED, NOT ASSUMED.
       System.Formats.Asn1 8.0.1 (GHSA-447r-wph3-92pm), System.Net.Http 4.3.4 (GHSA-7jgj-8wvc-jh57) and
       System.Text.RegularExpressions 4.3.1 (ReDoS) existed to force patched versions of packages the net8
       runtime subsumed but still advertised. Under net10 all three are provided in-box, so NuGet reports
       NU1510 ("automatically available ... remove the PackageReference") on every project that inherits
       them — 284 warnings, which is how they were noticed at all.
       THE CHECK THAT JUSTIFIES REMOVING THEM: they were deleted, `dotnet restore` was re-run, and the
       advisory list was read UNCAPPED — it is empty. The pins are not silencing anything any more, and a
       security pin that no longer corresponds to a live advisory is worse than none: it reads as active
       protection while doing nothing. Restore is the instrument; if a future runtime stops shipping a
       patched version, the advisory returns and the gate blocks, which is the control working. -->

  <!-- Newtonsoft.Json only where it actually surfaces (the 4 host/integration projects), scoped by project name
       so domain/module projects that use System.Text.Json stay free of it. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Acmp.Api' Or '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Acmp.Modules.Integrations.Webex' Or '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Acmp.Bootstrap' Or '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Acmp.Worker'">
    <PackageReference Include="Newtonsoft.Json" Version="13.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- SSH.NET <= 2025.1.0 (GHSA-q939-rpr3-3284, HIGH): ScpClient recursive download lets a malicious
       SERVER write arbitrary files on the client via crafted SCP filenames. Patched in 2026.0.0.
       Surfaces transitively through Testcontainers in the integration tests ONLY — it reaches no
       shipped image, and ACMP is not an SCP client — so the practical exposure here is a CI runner
       talking to the Docker-provisioned containers it started itself. Pinned rather than excluded
       anyway: the gate blocks High/Critical by design (C-SUP-02 / OQ-027), and "it's only tests" is
       exactly the argument that turns a blocking gate into an advisory one.
       Scoped to the project that actually pulls it, so nothing else acquires an SSH client. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Acmp.Integration.Tests'">
    <PackageReference Include="SSH.NET" Version="2026.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- DW-080 phase A (net8.0 -> net10.0): Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 10.0.0 pulls Microsoft.OpenApi
       2.0.0 transitively, which carries a HIGH advisory (GHSA-v5pm-xwqc-g5wc). ⚠ THE MIGRATION ITSELF
       INTRODUCED THIS — it did not exist on net8.0, where the 8.0.28 OpenApi package pulled a different
       tree. Pinned to the patched 2.x rather than left to the transitive resolution, exactly as the
       System.Formats.Asn1 and SSH.NET pins above: the CI dependency gate blocks on High/Critical by
       design (C-SUP-02 / OQ-027), and a runtime migration is precisely the change most likely to smuggle
       a new advisory in behind an unrelated green build.
       Deliberately 2.x and NOT 3.x: Microsoft.AspNetCore.OpenApi 10.0.0 is built against the 2.x surface,
       so a major bump here would be an API gamble to fix a CVE. Scoped to the one project that pulls it. -->
  <ItemGroup Condition="'$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Acmp.Api'">
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.OpenApi" Version="2.12.2" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!-- DW-080 phase A, the SECOND advisory cluster the migration introduced, and it is nothing to do with
       the OpenApi one above. Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.SqlServer 10.0.0 -> Microsoft.Data.SqlClient
       pulls System.Security.Cryptography.Xml 9.0.0, which carries EIGHT high advisories. It surfaces in
       every *.Infrastructure project rather than in one host, so it is pinned solution-wide here exactly
       as System.Formats.Asn1 is, not scoped.
       ⚠⚠ 10.0.0 IS ALSO VULNERABLE — "the next major is the patched one" is an assumption and it was
       WRONG here. Pinning 10.0.0 left all eight advisories standing; the fix is 10.0.11, the current
       patch. Verify a pin by re-running restore and reading the advisory list, never by the version
       number looking newer than the one the tool complained about.
       ⚠ HOW THIS CLUSTER WAS ALMOST MISSED, because the method matters more than the pin: the first
       restore was read through `sort -u | head -20`, which was full of NU1510 notices, and the conclusion
       drawn was "one new HIGH advisory". Re-read with NO cap there were two clusters. That is LL-015 /
       PE-599's truncated instrument, in the same session that cites them. Enumerate advisories uncapped.
       ⚠ NuGet reports NU1510 on THIS pin too — "automatically available, remove it" — and that advice is
       wrong here, which is why the warning is left standing rather than obeyed. The version the framework
       makes available is not what wins: SqlClient's transitive 9.0.0 does, and 9.0.0 is the vulnerable
       one. Removing this reference was tested IN THIS TREE and the advisories come straight back — 104
       reported hits across the Infrastructure projects, all naming 9.0.0. -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="System.Security.Cryptography.Xml" Version="10.0.11" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
